.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt RENAME 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rename ,
.Nm renameat ,
.Nm renameat2
.Nd rename a file within a mount
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In stdio.h
.Ft int
.Fn rename "const char *oldpath" "const char *newpath"
.Ft int
.Fn renameat "int olddirfd" "const char *oldpath" "int newdirfd" "const char *newpath"
.Ft int
.Fn renameat2 "int olddirfd" "const char *oldpath" "int newdirfd" "const char *newpath" "unsigned int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn rename
changes the name
.Fa oldpath
to
.Fa newpath .
Relative paths are resolved from the current working directory.
.Fn renameat
and
.Fn renameat2
resolve a relative
.Fa oldpath
from the directory
.Fa olddirfd
is open on and a relative
.Fa newpath
from
.Fa newdirfd ;
either may be
.Dv AT_FDCWD
for the current working directory, and an absolute path ignores its
directory argument.
The two parent directories must be on the same mount.
A final symbolic link is renamed or replaced as an entry; its target
is not followed.
.Pp
.Fn renameat2
with
.Dv RENAME_NOREPLACE
in
.Fa flags
refuses a
.Fa newpath
that exists with
.Er EEXIST ,
after a missing
.Fa oldpath
has given
.Er ENOENT
and before the
.Er ENOTDIR
of a slash after a name that is not a directory, as on Linux.
Both names are looked up under the mount's namespace lock, which every
change of a name on the mount takes, and the rename is made before the
lock is released, so no other process can create the new name between
the check and the rename.
With
.Fa flags
0 it is
.Fn renameat .
.Dv RENAME_EXCHANGE ,
.Dv RENAME_WHITEOUT
and any other flag return
.Er EINVAL
before either path is read; a Linux file system without them returns
.Er EINVAL
after the paths are resolved.
musl declares no
.Fn renameat2 ;
a program calls it through
.Fn syscall ,
with
.Dv RENAME_NOREPLACE
being 1.
.Pp
An existing regular-file destination may be replaced.
An open descriptor continues to refer to the file it opened, including
when that file's name is replaced.
On KRFFS, a replaced file with open descriptors becomes nameless and
its storage is freed at the last close.
It is absent from lookup and directory listings.
.Pp
Search permission is required on ancestor directories and write
permission on both parent directories.
The parent write checks precede lookup of the source, and of the
destination for
.Dv RENAME_NOREPLACE :
a caller without them gets
.Er EACCES
where Linux reports
.Er ENOENT
or
.Er EEXIST .
In a sticky directory, removing the source entry or replacing the
destination entry additionally requires effective user ID 0, ownership
of the directory, or ownership of the affected entry.
The KRFFS boot root is treated as mode 0755 and non-sticky.
.Pp
The KRFFS boot root refuses a directory source with
.Er EPERM
and a directory destination under a nondirectory source with
.Er EISDIR ,
so no rename there replaces a directory, one in use or not.
A mounted KRFFS volume also refuses directory renames, generally with
.Er EIO ;
an open directory destination gives
.Er EISDIR .
Ext2, FAT32, and exFAT can rename directories.
When one of them replaces a different directory, the destination must be
empty and not in use, as
.Xr rmdir 2
requires: a directory a descriptor or a working directory refers to
returns
.Er EBUSY ,
where Linux replaces it.
A directory cannot be moved into itself or below itself.
A directory and a nondirectory cannot replace each other.
.Pp
The root itself,
.Pa / ,
as either path is refused with
.Er EBUSY ,
and as the new path under
.Dv RENAME_NOREPLACE
with
.Er EEXIST ,
as on Linux, though before either path is walked, where Linux walks
both first.
A path whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
.Pa /.\&
and
.Pa /..\&
among them, returns
.Er EBUSY
once both paths are walked and found on one mount, and a
.Fa newpath
so named returns
.Er EEXIST
under
.Dv RENAME_NOREPLACE ,
as on Linux: before the parent write checks, and before the
.Er EROFS
of
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc .
A slash after either final name requires the source to be a directory:
anything else, a symbolic link to a directory among them, returns
.Er ENOTDIR ,
as on Linux.
Mount points cannot be renamed or replaced; under
.Dv RENAME_NOREPLACE
a
.Fa newpath
that exists, a mount point among them, returns
.Er EEXIST
first, whether or not
.Fa oldpath
is a mount point, as on Linux.
The synthetic
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc
namespaces are read-only: a rename in either returns
.Er EROFS
after the parent write checks.
On the KRFFS boot root, replacing a different file that backs an attached
loop device returns
.Er EBUSY .
This is a check on the destination, not the source.
Ext2 permits renaming or replacing a loop-backed file because the loop
device retains its inode.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 38 for
.Nm rename ,
302 for
.Nm renameat
and 353 for
.Nm renameat2 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success, or -1 on failure and set
.Va errno .
The raw kernel calls return a negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/fdstale.c
has a child open a file while its parent renames a replacement onto
that name.
The child reads the original contents through its descriptor;
opening the name again reads the replacement.
.Pa tests/musl/atcallmusl.c
renames through two directory descriptors, and checks that
.Dv RENAME_NOREPLACE
refuses an existing name and leaves both files as they were.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ENAMETOOLONG
.It Er EACCES
Search permission was denied on an ancestor directory, or write
permission was denied on either parent directory.
.It Er EBADF
A relative path's directory descriptor is not open.
.It Er EEXIST
.Dv RENAME_NOREPLACE
was given and
.Fa newpath
exists, is the root, or has
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
as its last component.
.It Er EBUSY
A path entered a mount being mounted or unmounted, either entry is a
mount point, either path is the root or has
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
as its last component, or on ext2, FAT32 or exFAT a destination
directory is in use: a descriptor or a working directory refers to it.
On the KRFFS boot root, a different destination file backs an attached
loop device.
.It Er EFAULT
A path cannot be read from user memory, or there is no current process.
.It Er EFBIG
An ext2 directory cannot grow further or a required block exceeds the
file system's addressing limit.
.It Er EINVAL
A destination has no leaf component, a
directory would move into itself or below itself, a file system
rejects the name or cannot supply required attributes, or
.Fn renameat2
was given a flag other than
.Dv RENAME_NOREPLACE .
.It Er EIO
File system data or metadata could not be read or written, or the
device was removed.
KRFFS also maps a refusal from its rename operation to this error.
.It Er EISDIR
A nondirectory would replace a directory on the KRFFS boot root, ext2,
FAT32, or exFAT.
A mounted KRFFS volume also gives this error for an open directory
destination.
.It Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Er EMLINK
An ext2 directory move would exceed the new parent's limit of 65000 links.
.It Er ENAMETOOLONG
A path, component, or symbolic-link expansion is too long.
The copied path, including any current-directory prefix and its
terminating null byte, must fit 4096 bytes.
.It Er ENOENT
A path is empty, a parent component is missing, or the source does not
exist on a file system that reports this error.
.It Er ENOMEM
A file system could not allocate memory needed for path resolution
or the rename.
.It Er ENOSPC
The destination directory cannot allocate space for the new entry.
.It Er ENOTDIR
A path component used as a directory is not a directory, a relative
path's directory descriptor is not open on a directory, a directory
source would replace a nondirectory on ext2, FAT32, or exFAT, or a
slash follows either final name and the source is not a directory.
.It Er ENOTEMPTY
A directory destination is not empty on ext2, FAT32, or exFAT.
.It Er EPERM
The source is a directory on the KRFFS boot root, or a sticky-directory
rule forbids removal or replacement.
.It Er EROFS
The mount is read-only, the file system has no rename operation,
either parent is synthetic, or an exFAT directory involved in the
rename cannot be modified.
.It Er EXDEV
The parent directories are on different mounts.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chmod 2 ,
.Xr link 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr unlink 2 ,
.Xr loop 4
